{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792171311,"nonce":"ub4/QRDfgbhsCkbRacuFcM6dyZE=","title":"A line breaks a word that fits, when the container's width came from rounding","message":"Reported from ChimpMonk's editor via ashui (ashui git-bug 743b470):\nshort button/trigger labels wrap to a second line even though the\nlabel's own box has enough width for them on one. Isolated repro\nbuilt: box width always comes out a whole number; wrapping or not\ndepends only on the exact string and font size, independent of\nanything else on screen, and single words never wrap.\n\nCause: break_lines's overflow check (crates/blinc_text/src/layout.rs)\ncompared the running line width against max_width with no tolerance.\nUpstream, a text node's box width commonly comes from Taffy's own\nround-to-nearest layout rounding (crates/.../taffy-0.14.0's\nround_layout, used by blinc_layout's measure callback as\n`known_dimensions`/`available_space` on a later pass) — which can\nland up to half a pixel under a label's true shaped width. When it\ndoes, the label no longer fits its own rounded box, and breaks at its\nlast space.\n\nFix: a half-pixel WRAP_EPSILON added to the overflow comparison in\nbreak_lines, since round-to-nearest can never move a value by more\nthan that. Confirmed blinc_text's and blinc_layout's full test suites\nstill pass (56+9+3 and 558 tests respectively, all green).","files":null}]}